{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Run the inventory web application on node2, listening on port 8080.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node2 directly showed an active, enabled service, a port-8080 listener, and successful health and inventory responses. After its required restart, its boot identity changed and the service, listener, health endpoint, and inventory data recovered.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7b122de0602f41c7","cmd-c14e37af26ee41d8","cmd-0e43dc70e53b4344","global:before_prepare:node2:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Run the inventory web application on node3, listening on port 8080.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node3 directly showed an active, enabled service, a port-8080 listener, and successful health and inventory responses. After its required restart, its boot identity changed and the service, listener, health endpoint, and inventory data recovered.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1e8c87b620ee4426","cmd-55ab3c6cec5c4bf1","cmd-28f7874fad954545","cmd-fd7b854cd5e84ed3","global:before_prepare:node3:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Configure node1 to accept HTTP on port 80 and load-balance requests across both node2 and node3.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node1 directly showed an active, enabled port-80 service and repeated requests alternating between node2 and node3. The root page and inventory API succeeded. Following node1's restart, its boot identity changed, the listener recovered, and balanced traffic was demonstrated again; the final eight-request check also alternated evenly across both backends.","evidence_ids":["cmd-efff123365a54ea4","cmd-acdaaa773f674607","cmd-c9c9a4228c3c4657","cmd-497afcd83fdd4ba3","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.97,"summary":"The deployment, service enablement, restarts, and verification commands all had clear roles in implementing the task or completing the mandatory restart protocol. Package health remained clean and no failed services were observed after execution. The only unnecessary mutations were creation of /var/lib/inventory on node2 and node3: the captured application never reads or writes those directories, and no later command removed them, so these low-risk empty directories apparently remained. The failed SSH probes merely observed bounded reboot outages and did not mutate state. Post-restart systemd-private temporary directories are expected runtime artifacts, not abandoned residue.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1cd8409aa4a5428b","cmd-20637be187ee4e12","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health","global:after_executor:node3:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All three material task outcomes are satisfied by direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol was also completed coherently on every managed node: each had a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity corroborated by lifecycle observations, recovered service/data, and final recovered load-balanced operation.","limitations":["The fixed lifecycle listening-socket observations were empty both before and after execution, so they do not independently corroborate the listeners; the executor's direct ss and HTTP checks provide the relevant evidence instead.","The node2 and node3 disruption-time load-balancer probes occurred after the backends had apparently already recovered, so they do not prove single-backend failover. Failover was not part of the public task contract.","No final filesystem inventory explicitly confirms whether the apparently unused /var/lib/inventory directories remained; their retention is inferred from their creation and the absence of a removal command."]}
